<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter12_file-errors-exceptions-and-applications">
    <title>File Errors, Exceptions, and Complete Applications</title>

    <p>
        File operations can fail because a file is missing, permissions are
        insufficient, storage is full, or a device reports an input/output
        failure. A program should distinguish expected conditions such as
        end-of-file from failures that require recovery or an error report.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_file-opening-exception">
        <title>Throwing when a file cannot be opened</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_file-opening-exception"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>
#include <ios>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

std::ifstream open_input_file(const std::string& filename) {
    std::ifstream input_file{filename};

    if (!input_file) {
        throw std::ios_base::failure{
            "Could not open " + filename
        };
    }

    return input_file;
}

int main() {
    try {
        std::ifstream input_file{
            open_input_file("scores.csv")
        };

        std::string line;
        while (std::getline(input_file, line)) {
            std::cout << line << '\n';
        }
    }
    catch (const std::ios_base::failure& error) {
        std::cout << "File operation failed: "
                  << error.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>
            The helper reports an opening failure immediately. The caller has
            the context to display a message, request another filename, select
            a default file, or stop the operation.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_stream-exception-mask">
        <title>Requesting stream exceptions</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_stream-exception-mask"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
std::ifstream input_file{"scores.csv"};
input_file.exceptions(std::ios::badbit);

if (!input_file) {
    throw std::ios_base::failure{"Could not open scores.csv"};
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>
            The exception mask selects which stream-state flags cause the stream
            to throw. Requesting <c>badbit</c> can report serious I/O failures
            while allowing ordinary end-of-file processing to remain a normal
            loop condition. Including <c>failbit</c> requires more careful
            handling because formatted input can set failure-related states at
            expected stopping points.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_exception-boundary">
        <title>Choosing an exception boundary</title>
        <p>
            Low-level code should provide enough information to explain the
            failure. Higher-level code should decide how to communicate with the
            user or whether to retry. Catch an exception at the lowest level
            that has enough context to respond meaningfully; do not catch and
            ignore it merely to make the program continue.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_complete-binary-application">
        <title>Designing a complete file application</title>
        <p>
            A reliable application defines its file format, checks every open and
            read operation, validates records, reports malformed input, and
            documents how data is represented. For binary files it must also
            specify field widths, byte order, layout, and version information.
            These rules allow a future program to determine whether it can safely
            read the file.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter12_8_file-exception">
        <statement><p>Why might <c>open_input_file()</c> throw?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice><statement><p>To guarantee that the operating system creates the file</p></statement><feedback><p>An exception reports failure; it does not create a missing file.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>To prevent the caller from continuing as though the file were available</p></statement><feedback><p>The caller can catch the exception and choose a meaningful response.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>To convert the file into a binary format</p></statement><feedback><p>Opening failure and file format are separate concerns.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>To make every later read succeed</p></statement><feedback><p>An exception does not repair later operations.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>To avoid checking the stream object anywhere</p></statement><feedback><p>The helper checks the stream before throwing.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_8_error-boundary">
        <statement><p>Select all sound file-error practices.</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Check whether a file opened successfully.</p></statement><feedback><p>Later operations should not assume an unavailable file is usable.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Handle an exception where the program has enough context to recover.</p></statement><feedback><p>The handling level should know what response is appropriate.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Ignore all file failures so the program continues.</p></statement><feedback><p>Continuing with invalid state can make the result unreliable.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Use exceptions to replace all ordinary loop conditions.</p></statement><feedback><p>Expected end-of-file conditions often belong in loop conditions.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Assume binary input is portable without documenting its format.</p></statement><feedback><p>Binary formats require explicit representation rules.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_8_exception-code" number="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="show">
        <statement><p>Arrange the blocks to open a file, throw on failure, and catch the exception.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>try {</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>    std::ifstream input_file{"scores.csv"};</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>    if (!input_file) {</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>        throw std::ios_base::failure{"Open failed"};</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>    }</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>}</cline></block>
            <block order="7"><cline>catch (const std::ios_base::failure&amp; error) {</cline></block>
            <block order="8"><cline>    std::cout &lt;&lt; error.what() &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="9"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>
</section>
